<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip V of Macedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">238–179 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> was Antigonid king of Macedonia from 221 to 179 BCE. He expanded Macedonian power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bylazora">Bylazora</a> in Paeonia. His siege of Abydos in 200 BCE helped bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a>, and his defeat at Cynoscephalae in 197 BCE confined him to Macedonia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip V of Macedon" width="320" height="522" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Livioandronico2013 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Philip_V_of_Macedon_in_Palazzo_Massimo_(Rome).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
